# Chapter 4: Settling

Settling is closure to a fixed point. It takes posited content and runs the lawful
closure process until the relevant orbit stabilizes, or until the reason it does not
stabilize becomes the phenomenon under study.

The closure ordinal of a settling is not a side detail. It is how the library
distinguishes strict-finite, Scott-continuous, transfinite-bounded, and aperiodic
regimes. Frontier mathematics often begins exactly where a finite explanation is
not enough to account for the closure behavior.

## Order is generated here

Infomaterial does not assume abstract order theory as a prior background. Order appears
when settling has produced closure, comparison, and fixed-point behavior. This is
why received order theory belongs downstream of settling in the documentation.

The manual should therefore introduce order as a generated structure: first the
closure process, then the comparison it induces, then the lattice operations and
fixed-point theorems recovered from that comparison.

## Regime is a measurement

The stages of the descent and the regimes of closure are related but not identical.
Articulating, predicating, positing, and settling are constructive acts. Strict,
Scott-continuous, transfinite-bounded, and aperiodic are measurements of how a
settling orbit behaves.

Keeping that distinction clear prevents a common confusion: the upstream stage is
not the same kind of thing as the downstream regime signature. Their recurrence is
mathematically meaningful precisely because they are not merely synonyms.

## The closure this chapter asks for

The reader must cross from valued content to stabilized comparison. Order is not
the hidden floor beneath the earlier chapters. It is recovered when closure makes
comparison and fixed-point behavior available.

The repair locator for this chapter is the closure orbit. If a later page claims
an order, lattice, sheaf, cohomology class, or regime without saying which
settling behavior generates it, the page is importing downstream vocabulary by
resemblance rather than deriving it.
